1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
This is CommonMesh: a WebMCP-native
workspace for community coordination.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,600
We begin from a deterministic reset—

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,000
seven open needs, fifteen available resources,
and no plan or commitments.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,500
An organizer must reconcile quantities,
skills, licences, schedules,

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,500
travel, and accessibility.

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,500
The agent handles that comparison work,

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,000
while CommonMesh keeps every real-world commitment
visible and human-controlled.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,800
Nine structured tools expose the domain directly:
seven reads and two writes.

9
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:37,400
The agent can inspect, validate, stage,
and commit.

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:41,000
But approval, reset, and resource availability
remain human-only.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,500
The agent reads stable identifiers
and structured constraints

12
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,800
instead of guessing from pixels.

13
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:55,000
It validates the assignments, then stages—
not executes—the proposal

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
through the same state store
used by the interface.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,500
The proposal covers every need and exposes
its assignments, metrics, and revision.

16
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,600
It also exposes validation
and a SHA-256 digest.

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:15,300
Notice that projected coverage
is one hundred percent

18
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:20,000
while live coverage is still zero.
Nothing has executed.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Even when explicitly asked to commit early,
the domain layer rejects the write

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,500
with APPROVAL_REQUIRED
and records the blocked attempt.

21
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:36,000
The agent cannot approve itself.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
I approve this exact digest in the visible UI.

23
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,500
Approval still does not execute it.

24
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:50,000
Now the agent commits only
that approved digest.

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
CommonMesh recomputes it, revalidates the plan,
and activates the assignments.

26
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Real plans change. As the human,
I mark the assigned van unavailable.

27
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Only its dependent assignment is disrupted;
the other seven commitments stay active.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The agent inspects the changed revision
and stages the smallest valid repair.

29
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,500
CommonMesh proves the impact:
seven existing assignments preserved,

30
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:31,000
one replaced, and full projected
coverage restored.

31
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,800
After a second exact-digest approval,
the agent commits the repair.

32
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:44,000
We end at seven of seven needs covered,
eight active assignments,

33
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
and a complete human-and-agent audit trail.

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
CommonMesh gives agents useful autonomy
without giving away human authority.
